 "result": "\n\nBoth findings addressed:\n\n1. **Declared the primary calendar as a decoy** — it's pre-existing and I can't alter it (seeding another `calendars` row with id `jordan.lee@northwind.example` would hit the same PK collision), and as the reader observed it passes c2 (LA time) and c3 (data-owned by Jordan). It's now a declared `A:Calendar.summary` / **F0** decoy: \"the actor's own primary calendar\" — on LA time and data-owned by the actor, but not a Berlin trip calendar. Its mutation drops the name filter (`DROP f_name`), which selects it while the original query doesn't. It's a genuine lazy-pick trap: an agent that ignores the name and grabs \"a calendar I own in LA time\" would land on the primary at the top of its list.\n2. **Reworded the request's first sentence** from \"My trip to Berlin starts next month\" to \"I'm planning my Berlin trip and want the calendar to show Berlin local time.\" The old phrasing derived a concrete date (July 2018) that no record field can support, so it could never be a faithful condition; the new phrasing keeps the same motivation with no derivable time claim. Conditions c1–c3 are unchanged and now exactly match the request's identifying phrases.\n\nNothing else changed — the target, the four original decoys, the query, and the write are as before.",
